{"author":{"id":"dec876858d28babbadcead4137861bf586626b8518cbce9c5f4fc52ac3b424b8"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1790966799,"nonce":"rhKPvq4UUaThRiwpk6GjDrk4RW8=","title":"A last-write-wins process slot must not be the only owner of an installed Arc; the","message":"`blinc_runtime::fsm` has two process-wide installs, and both were wrong\nin the same way. The machine driver kept its strong `Arc` only in a\nlast-write-wins slot with a `Weak` in a thread-local; the guard\ndispatcher had a slot and nothing else. So the next `BlincDsl::new()`\nANYWHERE in the process took over: the driver answered\n`machine_for(\"Tally\")` with \"not a fiber function in the loaded\nprogram\", and a transition action resolved its symbol in a program that\nnever declared it, leaving the write silently undone. As test flakes:\n~5% and ~40%. In a host holding two DSL instances it is not a flake, it\nis the behaviour.\n\n**Why:** a `Weak` in a thread-local is right (a TLS must not own the\nruntime Arc, see [[gotcha-runtime-lock-across-jit]]), but something with\nthe correct lifetime still has to hold the strong reference, and a\nlast-write-wins slot is not it. The object that built the thing owns it;\nthe slot is a fallback for threads that built nothing.\n\n**How to apply:** when installing into a global slot, keep your own\nstrong reference, and give the lookup a per-thread preference so a\nprocess running several instances asks its own. Test shape: install from\ninstance A, build instance B on another thread, then assert A's thread\nstill resolves A's program. Deterministic, unlike looping the flake.\nAlso: `machine_for`'s failure was invisible because the test file\ninstalled no `tracing_subscriber`, so a warn went nowhere.","files":null}]}